From: substrate
Subject: xkb: guard _XkbCreateIndicatorMap against junk sli->names/maps

Running `reset(1)` from an xterm asks XKB to reset indicator maps,
which routes through _XkbSetNamedIndicator -> _XkbCreateIndicatorMap.
The function calls XkbFindSrvLedInfo, checks the returned pointer
non-NULL, and then walks sli->names[] / sli->maps[] without
validating that those pointers themselves are real.

Substrate sometimes leaves the per-device LED info incompletely
initialised, leaving sli->names pointing into low memory.  The
"find first unused indicator" loop then walks off into unmapped
pages -- seen in a kernel trap with addr=0x000010CB at
_XkbCreateIndicatorMap+0xAC (the `mov (%edx), %ebx` inside the
post-increment iteration).

Apply the same low-pointer-reject pattern used in the fb guards:
if either array pointer is below 0x10000, log once and return
BadAlloc.  The client gets a clean XKB error reply instead of
losing the X server.

--- a/xkb/xkb.c
+++ b/xkb/xkb.c
@@ -3408,6 +3408,24 @@
     if (!sli)
         return BadAlloc;
 
+    /* Substrate hardening: reject low-junk pointers in sli->names /
+     * sli->maps before the loops below walk through them.  Seen in
+     * the wild when `reset(1)` from an xterm asks XKB to reset
+     * indicator maps and the per-device LED info was incompletely
+     * initialised -- the loop ran off the end of the names array and
+     * faulted at addr=0x000010CB.  Returning BadAlloc here means the
+     * client gets an error reply instead of the server dying. */
+    if ((uintptr_t)sli->names < 0x10000u || (uintptr_t)sli->maps < 0x10000u) {
+        static int warned = 0;
+        if (!warned) {
+            warned = 1;
+            ErrorF("_XkbCreateIndicatorMap: bogus sli (names=%p maps=%p) "
+                   "-- returning BadAlloc instead of walking junk.\n",
+                   (void *)sli->names, (void *)sli->maps);
+        }
+        return BadAlloc;
+    }
+
     map = _XkbFindNamedIndicatorMap(sli, indicator, &led);
 
     if (!map) {
